\begin_layout Standard
Graphene is an isolated basal plane of graphite, one atom thick and forming
 a hexagonal array of carbon atoms connected by strong covalent bonds (see
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:GraphENE_crystal_structure"

\end_inset

A,B).
 The valence electrons of each carbon atom are sp
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2 
\end_layout

\end_inset

hybridised, allowing an atom to bond to three nearest neighbour atoms and
 have electrons left over in an unhybridised 2p
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
z
\end_layout

\end_inset

 orbital for conduction across the planar lattice.
 (This can be contrasted with insulating diamond, where carbon is sp
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
3
\end_layout

\end_inset

 hybridised and all valence electrons are engaged in bonding to the four
 nearest carbon atoms.) Finally, the two atoms in the unit cell of graphene
 belong to two independent sublattices, so that any given atom is bonded
 only to those in the opposite sublattice.
 This has important implications for charge carriers in graphene.
 The band structure arising from the combination of these orbitals and the
 hexagonal sublattices is unique and underpins the exotic nature of charge
 carriers in graphene.
 Considering the reciprocal lattice of graphene (shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:GraphENE_crystal_structure"

\end_inset

C) and using the tight binding approximation, the electronic dispersion
 of graphene can be plotted across the Brillioun zone.
 Where the conduction and valence bands meet at the K points, there are
 regions of linear dispersion which form a zero band gap about the Fermi
 level.
 Thus, graphene is a zero band gap semiconductor or a semimetal.
 These regions have been named 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Dirac cones
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and their effect on charge carriers moving through graphene, and resulting
 phenomena are explained next.
 One final note on the structure of graphene is that a fully relaxed structure
 holds static wrinkles which oscillate over tens of nanometre.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "meyer2007thestructure"

\end_inset

 It is thought that this mechanism stabilises graphene (and other 2D materials),
 allowing it to exist as a single sheet of atoms.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "fasolino2007intrinsic,geim2007therise"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 2004 Nobel laureates Prof Andre Geim and Prof Konstantin Novoselov were
 the first to successfully isolate and characterise free standing graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2004electric"

\end_inset

, although it should be acknowledged that monolayer graphite was known prior
 to this
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "soule1968directbasalplane,geim2007therise"

\end_inset

.
 They did this using the 'Scotch tape' method, where micromechanical cleavage
 is achieved using sticky tape to pull apart thick highly oriented pyrolytic
 graphite until it is atomically thin.
 The first electronic transport measurements not only showed that graphene's
 band structure has Dirac cones at the K-points of the Brillouin zone, and
 that charge carriers close to the centre of the cone behave as massless
 Dirac fermions, but that the anomalous quantum Hall effect and a non-trivial
 Berry's phase could be experimentally measured in high quality graphene
 flakes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2005twodimensional,zhang2005experimental"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These phenomena all arise from two fundamental features of graphene's electronic
 structure.
 Firstly, charge carriers in graphene are chiral: Graphene has two sublattices
 which both contribute to the wavefunction of charge carriers, effectively
 burdoning them with an additional quantum number 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

psuedospin
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Psuedospin sets charge carriers in graphene apart from other two dimensional
 electron gases as this additional property suppresses the backscattering
 of carriers.
 Broadly, this means that exotic quantum mechanical phenomena are more likely
 to be observed in graphene than in more traditional condensed matter systems
 where scattering can dominate measurements.
 A surprising manifestation of this effect is the Klein paradox, where carriers
 will always propogate through a sharp barrier if the angle of incidence
 is 0° to the barrier normal.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "katsnelson2006chiraltunnelling,young2009quantum,wilmart2014akleintunneling"

\end_inset

 Graphene also has a unique electronic bandstructure: a gapless, linear
 energy spectrum for electrons and holes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2009graphene,castroneto2009theelectronic"

\end_inset

 Charge carriers in normal semiconductors can be described by a relatively
 straightforward Schrödinger equation, where the energy and effective mass
 varies parabolically.
 In graphene the Schrödinger equation is not applicable to the linear energy
 spectrum.
 Instead the charge carriers must be described by a 2D relativistic Dirac
 equation as they are massless about the linear region; thus massless Dirac
 fermions.
 This is the unique case of monolayer graphene.
 In bilayer graphene an interesting combination of Dirac and Schrödinger
 equations results in massive Dirac fermions with four possible psuedospin
 states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These revelations have propelled graphene to academic and mainstream fame,
 with the original Science paper recieving over 21,000 citations, placing
 it in the top 100 most cited papers.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vannoorden2014thetop"

\end_inset

 Rapid progress in the field revealed monolayer graphene to have carrier
 mobilities of up to 200,000 cm
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2 
\end_layout

\end_inset

V
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1 
\end_layout

\end_inset

s
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

at low temperature in the highest quality devices 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2008intrinsic,bolotin2008ultrahigh"

\end_inset

, but, more interestingly for electronic devices, mobilities upwards of
 15,000 cm
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2 
\end_layout

\end_inset

V
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

 s
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

at room temperature
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2007therise"

\end_inset

.
 It was not long before it was realised that the thin silicon dioxide substrate,
 which was relied upon to locate graphene under the optical microscope
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "blake2007makinggraphene"

\end_inset

 and insulate it from the silicon back gate
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2005twodimensional,zhang2005experimental"

\end_inset

, was breaking up the 2D electron gas in graphene through surface roughness,
 small areas of inhomogenous surface charging and impurities.
 The device performance was significantly improved by inserting a thin layer
 of insulating hexagonal boron nitride (hBN) between graphene and the substrate.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2010boronnitride"

\end_inset

This heralded a step change in graphene device quality and was one of the
 first instances of a van der Waals' heterostructure.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2013vander"

\end_inset


\end_layout

