\begin_layout Standard
Bulk hBN glues inert sheets of hexagonal boron and nitrogen lattices together
 with van der Waals' bonds, similar to graphite.
 The lattice parameters of hBN have values of 
\series bold
a 
\series default
= 2.50 Å and 
\series bold
c
\series default
 = 6.66 Å, which differ only slightly from graphite gives a lateral lattice
 mismatch of ~1.6%.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "woods2014commensurateincommensurate"

\end_inset

 hBN prefers AA' stacking, where every boron (nitrogen) atom sits above
 a nitrogen (boron) atom of the neighbouring lattice (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:hBN crystal Structure"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "constantinescu2013stacking"

\end_inset

 This stacking is highly unfavourable in graphite, which prefers AB (Bernal)
 stacking, yet the polar nature of hBN bonding allows the 
\series bold
c
\series default
 lattice parameters for both materials to be nearly identical.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hod2012graphite"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
hBN is a dielectric and , unlike the majority of other 2D materials, this
 useful property does not require quantum confinement to be realised.
 This means that, as we will discuss later, hBN flakes of different thickness
 can be used to separate different conducting components of a heterostructure
 and prevent shorting.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2012electron"

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, when thin sheets of graphene are placed on top of hBN and
 annealed, surface contaminants pool together in bubbles in what has become
 known as the self-cleaning phenomenon.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lui2009ultraflat,haigh2012crosssectional,georgiou2013vertical"

\end_inset

 Self-cleaning arises from the van der Waals' attraction between hBN and
 graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "khestanova2016graphene"

\end_inset

 and results in large areas of atomically clean interfaces, proving vital
 for high quality measurements.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2010boronnitride"

\end_inset

 These humble properties have allowed some of the most exciting physical
 phenomena to be realised, including negative differential conductance
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2013resonant"

\end_inset

, Hofstadter’s butterfly in bilayer graphene 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2013hofstadters"

\end_inset

 and Klein tunneling of the chiral Dirac fermions.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "katsnelson2006chiraltunnelling,young2009quantum,wilmart2014akleintunneling"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Other van der Waals' materials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are an estimated 500 van der Waals' materials which hold the potential
 to yield large 2D flakes like graphene.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gibney2015thesuper"

\end_inset

 Their properties, and the effects of quantum confinement, are yet to be
 predicted for many of them.
 The 2D crystals that have been isolated since the discovery of graphene
 show a diverse spectrum of electronic properties including metals, semiconducto
rs, insulators and superconductors.
 Some of the most recently isolated are silicene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "zhao2016riseof"

\end_inset

, germanene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "davila2014germanene,derivaz2015continuous"

\end_inset

, stanene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "zhu2015epitaxial"

\end_inset

, and phosphorene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "liu2014phosphorene"

\end_inset

 (atomically thin layers of silicon, germanium, tin and phosphorus, respectively
), to name but a few.
 Furthermore, some of these 2D materials are expected to have various structural
 forms available when atomically thin.
 For example, phosphorene is predicted to have two different phases when
 monolayer; dubbed blue and black phosphorene, although only the latter
 has been realised experimentally.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "li2014blackphosphorus,liu2014phosphorene,zhu2014semiconducting,wu2015atomicand"

\end_inset

 Many of these materials are unstable under standard conditions and so research
 has largely been limited to those that are stable (i.e.
 most of the transition metal dichalcogenides and some metal oxides)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2013vander"

\end_inset

, although specialist handling strategies have being developed for unstable
 materials (see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Making-and-transferring graphene"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cao2015quality"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One very promising class of 2D materials are the transition metal dichalcogenide
s (TMDCs).
 TMDCs are metallic or semiconducting crystals of formula MX
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, where M is a transition metal (e.g.
 molybdenum, tungsten, niobium, etc.) and X is a chalcogen (sulfur, selenium
 or tellerium).
 Similarly to graphene and hBN, a TMDC has sheets of hexagonal crystal structure
 with strong in-plane bonding.
 Each sheet is stacked on top of another and held by van der Waals' forces.
 However, unlike graphene and hBN, they are not one atomic layer but three,
 with a sheet of transition metal atoms encapsulated by two sheets of chalcogen
 atoms.
 The most popular TMDCs are currently MoS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, WS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, MoSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

and WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 due to them having an indirect semiconducting band gap in bulk crystals
 and a direct gap in monolayer crystals.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "mak2010atomically,splendiani2010emerging,zhang2014directobservation"

\end_inset

 The direct band gaps of the popular semiconducting TMDCs range from roughly
 1.5 - 2.1 eV
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2012electronics"

\end_inset

, which correspond to near infrared - yellow photon energies in the visible
 spectrum.
 Furthermore, these monolayers are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

multivalley semiconductors
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, meaning they have favourable and distinct localised states which a charge
 carrier wavefunction can occupy.
 These states appear as valleys at the K points of the Brillouin zone (K
 and -K in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Liu_introTMDCstruc"

\end_inset

), with differences between valleys arising from a lack of inversion symmetry
 in the crystal structure of monolayer TMDCs.
 A carrier can be confined to one of these valleys and so gains an extra
 constraint: a 
\emph on
k
\emph default
-valley index (also known as valley degree of freedom or valley pseudospin).
 In addition to this, semiconducting TMDCs show strong spin-orbit coupling
 which arises from the valence electron states originating from d-orbitals.
 This results in spin up and spin down carriers having different energies
 as spin degeneracy is removed from the conduction and valence bands.
\end_layout

