\emph on
P(r)
\emph default
 is the complex point spread function of the electron microscope
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "nellist2000theprinciples"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
I_{coh}(r)=|\psi(r)\bigotimes P(r)|^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the general case for coherent imaging, used in both BF STEM and
 high resolution TEM (HRTEM).
 If we assume that the specimen is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

weak phase object
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, i.e.
 the specimen is thin enough so that only a small phase shift occurs as
 the electron wavefunction passes through the object, it can be shown that
 the intensity of coherent scattering varies with spatial frequency 
\emph on
