 of atomic scale structures and geometries.
 We will now explore complementary techniques which further extend the chemical
 analysis of the STEM instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Analytical spectroscopic methods: EDXS
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Analytical-EDXS"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When incident electrons inelastically scatter through an electron cloud,
 if the accelerating voltage is high enough, core electrons of the atom
 can be kicked out to leave a hole.
 The hole is quickly filled by an electron from a higher energy orbital,
 which requires the emission of an X-ray photon.
 The photon will have the energy difference between the electrons initial
 state and when it has filled the hole.
 This photon energy is characteristic of the atom it radiates from, as the
 core orbitals are unscreened from the nucleus charge and so give unique
 photon energies for different elements.
 We can use this as a fingerprint to determine the composition of a specimen.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

 Unlike many TEM techniques, EDXS requires a thick specimen to generate
 enough counts so that the signal is detectible above the background X-rays
 (named the bremsstrahlung and arising from the deceleration of electrons
 through the specimen).
 Furthermore, X-rays from light elements have very low photon energies and
 are very difficult to get good statistics with EDXS and so are usually
 consigned to detection by EELS if possible (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Analytical-EELS"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
EDXS operates differently in CTEM and STEM.
 In CTEM the entire area illuminated by the broad beam contributes to one
 spectrum, from which the composition of the specimen is inferred.
 In STEM, a spectrum can be acquired from each probe position.
 This is known as Spectrum Imaging (SI) and is very powerful as the local
 composition of nanostructures can be determined by mapping characteristic
 peak intensities across the image.
 Furthermore EDXS SI can be done in tandem with HAADF STEM imaging and BF
 imaging or EELS SI to gather multiple signals simultaneously for the same
 region of specimen.
 As X-ray emission occurs in all directions EDXS is not limited to transmission
 techniques and is also used ubiquitously in scanning electron microscope
 systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Analytical spectroscopic methods: EELS
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Analytical-EELS"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) extends the use of the electrons
 in the microscope from purely imaging to giving us extensive elemental
 and chemical information about the specimen.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "brydson2001electron"

\end_inset

 Incident electrons can experience a multitude of inelastic scattering events
 on passing through the specimen, resulting in them losing the initial energy
 gained in the electron gun.
 We can seperate out these electrons by their energy loss using an electromagnet
ic prism to form a spectrum.
 The loss energies associated with different scattering phenomena are all
 present in the spectrum.
 As we require the direct beam and weakly scattered electrons about this
 the bright field detector is removed from the optical axis when using the
 EELS detector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The low loss region (0 - 50 eV) of the spectrum contains the zero loss peak,
 which contains all elastically scattered electrons from the specimen, and
 plasmon peak(s).
 If the specimen is thick (e.g.
 > 90 nm for silicon) two or more plasmon peaks will be present and the
 specimen will not be suitable for EELS analysis (although EDXS count rate
 will not be problem, see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Analytical-EDXS"

\end_inset

) For suitably thin specimen, where only one scattering event is present,
 there will only be a single plasmon peak.
 Taking the ratio of this peak and the zero loss peak allows you to calculate
 the mean free path of an electron through the specimen.
 This can be converted into specimen thickness using the log-ratio method
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "malis1988eelslogratio,iakoubovskii2008thickness"

\end_inset

.
 It is also useful to record the low loss region for correction of the high
 loss region as not only does the energy dispersion shift during acquisition
 but the core loss EELS edges are convolved with the high intensity peaks
 in the loss region and should ideally be deconvolved for quantitative analysis.
 Recording both low and high loss regions is known as dual-EELS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The high loss region is far less intense than the low loss, however it contains
 the most useful signals coming from the specimen.
 A core-loss edge corresponds to a scattering process where a core electron
 has been promoted to an unoccupied state just above the fermi level.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "egerton2011electron"

\end_inset

 The position of the edge can be used to identify the element and the fine
 structure of the edge (i.e.
 small peaks after the edge onset) can provide information on the chemical
 environment of the species
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "alexander2008thereal"

\end_inset

.
 For excitations with low cross sections the signal can be very low, especially
 for higher energy excitations with a gradual edge onset.
 EELS is particularly useful for identifying light elements and is highly
 complementary to EDXS.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Electron beam induced effects
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Electron-beam-induced"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the microscope and specimen are both optimised, the changes induced in
 the specimen by the electron beam are the next thing to limit the quality
 of the image or spectrum image in STEM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whatever specimen you are looking at, the most common problem is contamination
 build up during imaging.
 This has been an issue in TEM for a very long time
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "egerton1976directmeasurement"

\end_inset

, however the situation has improved with advances in vacuum systems.
 Nevetheless, it is still very common in all electron microscopy methods
 to experience a gradual change in image contrast with increased electron
 fluence, as mobile carbon contamination moves across the surface of the
 specimen into the highly charged area under the electron beam.
 Strategies to combat this are cooling the specimen, 'beam showering' and
 careful oxidative plasma cleaning of the specimen
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "mitchell2015contamination"

\end_inset

.
 For extremely sensitive specimen, heating by the electron beam can also
 induce changes, however these are estimated to be on the order of 1 °C
 for a suitably thin sample in CTEM and STEM
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "egerton2004radiation"

\end_inset

.
 A far bigger problem is direct damage induced by the electron beam.
 Generally this takes the form of knock-on damage or radiolytic damage.
 Knock-on damage is when an incident electron collides with an atomic nucleus,
 either irreversibly displacing it from its original position or ejecting
 it from the specimen.
 The cross sections for knock-on damage for light elements, such as carbon,
 boron and nitrogen, are significant even in covalently bound systems
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "meyer2012accurate"

\end_inset

 and the electron beam energy should be reduced so that incident electrons
 do not breach the sputtering threshold of the species 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "egerton2010basicquestions"

\end_inset

.
 Radiolysis is damage initiated by ionising an atom in the specimen, and
 where the hole is not filled within the timescale of phonons.
 This results in the weakened bond being torn apart by lattice vibrations,
 which in turn leads to a release of secondary electrons and generating
 a local cascade of radiolytic damage
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "egerton2013control"

\end_inset

.
 Radiolysis is mostly confined to semiconductors and insulators, as the
 dissociated electron cloud in metals almost instantaneously fills any holes
 generated.
 Radiolytic damage can be reduced somewhat by cooling the specimen to reduce
 thermal vibrations, making the specimen thinner, or increasing the electron
 beam energy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

.
 The final electron beam effect we will discuss is electrostatic charging.
 A strong electrostatic field can be induced in thin, insulating specimen
 subject to a STEM probe.
 This arises from the generation of charged species and ejection of secondary
 electrons from the specimen and has been shown to have different polarity
 in different regions of a wedge specimen
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jiang2016beamdamage"

\end_inset

.
 Anion and cation species are made mobile by the electrostatic field and
 can cause breakdown of the specimen structure
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "egerton2014choiceof"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All these affects were encountered in this work due to the beam sensitive
 nature of many of the components of atomically thin van der Waals' materials,
 many of which were bulk hBN dielectric.
 Characterisation of the extent of beam damage from FIB and STEM irradition
 is demonstrated in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:FIB-induced-limits"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Advanced signal processing techniques for image and spectrum datasets
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Advanced-signal-processing"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To get quantitative information from raw TEM data, some data processing
 is usually required to .
 We will briefly cover some of the techniques used in this work.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A common technique in TEM imaging is to convert an image to a fast fourier
 transform (FFT), filtering or masking in the frequency domain, and using
 an inverse fast fourier transform to recover the desired data in the image.
 This is usually done with the intention of removing low frequency noise
 from the image
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

 or subtracting the information from a specific lattice in order to better
 interpret the image (usually done in conjunction with image simulation)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "meyer2011experimental"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For direct processing of images some common techniques are gaussian blur,
 for smoothing spatial fluctuations; thresholding to generate a binary image
 to isolate specific features; edge detection; Hough transforms, for detecting
 straight lines; and skeletonisation, which reduces a multi-pixel structure
 down to a single thread of pixels
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vanderwalt2014scikitimage"

\end_inset

.
 The last technique is very useful for quantitative analysis in TEM images
 as it allows the position of a lattice fringe to be expressed as an absolute
 pixel coordinate, and is used by the 'PyroMaN' software
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dacosta2015investigating"

\end_inset

 to extract quantitative information from HRTEM lattice fringes
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "mironov2015electron,freeman2016onthe"

\end_inset

.
 Many of these processing techniques have been used in this work on HAADF
 STEM images, however each one is applied image-wise with close supervision
 of the outcome for each technique, and not as a black box algorithm applied
 to a stack of images (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Image-processing-methods"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Multidimensional-data analysis is also possible for both STEM images and
 spectrum images
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pena2016hyperspy"

\end_inset

.
 A dataset only requires two fundimental dimensions: the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

signal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 axis, which plots the amplitude of some signal or measurement; and a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

navigation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 axis, which tells us over what metric the signal fluctuates.
 More than one type of axis can be assigned e.g.
 a greyscale TEM image has two navigation axes and one signal axis, a simple
 spectrum has only one of each, and a spectrum image has three navigation
 axes (two for spatial probe position, one for energy) and one signal axis
 (X-ray or electron counts).
 By assigning axes correctly we can apply multidimensional-data analysis
 effectively to any dataset and extract the components of the signal we
 desire and map them across the navigation axes.
 A common branch of multidimensional-data analysis is blind source separation
 which attempts to disentangle a set of variables which outpopulate any
 relationships between them
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "acharyya2008anew"

\end_inset

.
 Machine learning algorithms such as Principle Component Analysis (PCA)
 can distinguish parts of a complex signal based on their varience and,
 with some human input, be used to remove noise and identify signal components
 with physical meaning
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "hyvarinen2000independent"

\end_inset

.
 PCA has been successfully applied to multidimensional datasets in microscopy,
 such as EELS and EDXS SIs
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "delapena2011mapping,rossouw2015multicomponent,shin2016synthesis"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(S)TEM analysis of 2D materials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will now review TEM and STEM imaging of 2D materials, covering plan view
 (perpendicular to the basal planes) and cross sectional (down the basal
 planes) imaging.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(S)TEM imaging of 2D materials has been crucial in the rapidly expanding
 field of 2D materials as many techniques (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Characterisation-of-vdWshets"

\end_inset

) do not yield directly interpretable information about the atomic structure
 of these materials.
 In fact, one of the first TEM studies was to use simple TEM diffraction
 methods to calibrate the Raman spectrum of graphene so that flake thickness
 and quality could be determined quickly and non-destructively
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferrari2006ramanspectrum,meyer2007thestructure"

\end_inset

.
 The properties of graphene make it an ideal specimen for TEM techniques
 as it is the thinnest possible material, is crystalline and a brilliant
 electronic conductor.
 Early imaging of pristine graphene showed the intrinsic corrugations of
 the structure
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "meyer2007thestructure"

\end_inset

, which soon led on to studying defect structures in the graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "meyer2008directimaging"

\end_inset

, as well as adatoms and adsorbates on the graphene surface
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "meyer2008imaging"

\end_inset

.
 Defects in graphene are particularly interesting as they can propogate
 through the lattice via simple bond rotation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kotakoski2014imaging,zan2012graphene"

\end_inset

, and their concentration creates glass-like 2D amorphous structures of
 5-7 carbon rings
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "banhart2011structural,kotakoski2011frompoint"

\end_inset

.
 Analysis of dopants in graphene are of great interest and the valency of
 adatoms on graphene has been explored with EELS spectroscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ramasse2013probing"

\end_inset

 and their movement through the graphene lattice tracked at atomic resolution
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "susi2014siliconcarbon"

\end_inset

.
 Graphene can also be dynamically manipulated using damage from the electron
 beam to induce morphological changes such as sputtering to produce graphene
 nanoribbons
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "rodriguez-manzo2016insitu,wang2016fabrication"

\end_inset

 to conversion into fullerene balls
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chuvilin2010directtransformation"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It can safely be said that graphene is now fully characterised within the
 limits of TEM methods, however its status as the ideal specimen extends
 beyond its own characterisation.
 Graphene is highly useful as a continuous carbon film across TEM grids,
 upon which specimen can be suspended and placed in the path of the electron
 beam
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "meyer2008imaging,nair2010graphene"

\end_inset

.
 The hexagonal lattice structure of graphene may prove critical in allowing
 dose sensitive molecules to be imaged by TEM using sampling and reconstruction
 techniques
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "meyer2014atomicstructure"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The other popular 2D materials are not as ideal for TEM analysis, as many
 of them are either semiconductors or insulators.
 Early studies used this to watch defect structures evolve under the electron
 beam in hBN
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jin2009fabrication,meyer2009selective,kotakoski2010electron"

\end_inset

 and semiconducting TMDCs
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "komsa2012twodimensional,komsa2013frompoint"

\end_inset

, however questions remained about the distribution of intrinsic defects
 within these materials.
 Following the trends in graphene TEM imaging, the electron beam was used
 to sculpt other 2D materials into nanowires
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "cretu2014experimental,vierimaa2016phosphorene,masihdas2016controlled"

\end_inset

, which proved particularly easy for TMDCs as the calcogen species are readily
 sputtered to leave bridging metallic nanowires
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "liu2011identification,lin2014flexible"

\end_inset

.
 To probe the pristine structure of TMDCs it was found that they can withstand
 far higher electron doses if fuly encapsulated by graphene sheets
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "zan2013control,algara-siller2013thepristine"

\end_inset

.
 This has since been extended to encapsulating liquid systems in graphene
 and observing the effects of van der Waals' pressures on trapped compounds
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "algara-siller2015squareice,vasu2016vander2,khestanova2016universal"

\end_inset

.
 The latest application of this has been to study the effects of confinement
 on water flow in nanocapillaries, as presented in Chapter 6
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "radha2016molecular2"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whilst the majority of information can be gathered from plan view TEM for
 monolayer flakes, structures composed of multiple layers of the same or
 dissimilar crystals require cross sectional imaging to be fully characterised.
 The main motivation for this is to directly confirm the thickness of each
 component, show that the interfaces are atomically sharp with no elemental
 intermixing between components, and that large areas are free from defects
 and contamination.
 Cross sectioning can also reveal growth nucleation sites for bottom-up
 fabricated methods (such as epitxial growth and CVD methods).
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "norimatsu2014growthof"

\end_inset

 Bottom-up methods lead to heterostructures which cover very large areas
 of substrate and so are suitable for more tradition TEM specimen preparation
 methods which are not site specific
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

.
 Cross sectioning by mechanical polishing or dimple grinding, followed by
 Ar ion milling, has been shown to be reasonably successful for a range
 of chemical vapour and epitaxially grown 2D materials, and give good quality
 HRTEM and HAADF STEM analysis
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "norimatsu2009transitional,borysiuk2010transmission,norimatsu2014epitaxial,norimatsu2014growthof,saito2015selforganized,palacio2015atomicstructure"

\end_inset

.
 Cross section specimen of heterostructures can also be prepared using a
 microtome and glued to a TEM grid, however lamellae produced thus far are
 not suitable for high resolution TEM methods
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "zhang2014vertical"

\end_inset

.
 Mechanical polishing techniques have many advantages over FIB preparation,
 as large, thin areas can be produced without the passivation layer and
 ion implantation associated with FIB techniques
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "burnett2015largevolume"

\end_inset

.
 Nevertheless FIB specimen preparation is overwhelmingly popular in the
 literature for heterostructures fabricated using bottom-up methods.
 Examples range from epitaxially grown graphene on silicon carbide
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "liu2012epitaxial,sediri2015atomically,pierucci2016bandalignment,lin2014atomically,lin2014directsynthesis"

\end_inset

 to chemically deposited hBN and TMDC heterostructures
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "sutter2013scalable2,lin2014atomically,lin2014directsynthesis,hallam2015controlled,wang2015bnenabled,que2015graphenetextendashsilicon,pierucci2016bandalignment,woods2016onestep,li2016largearea,yue2015hfse2thin,browning2015largearea,lu2016atomicand,vishwanath2016controllable,lin2015atomically,lu2015atomicresolution"

\end_inset

.
 Conversely, heterostructures composed of mechanically exfoliated flakes
 are typically only a few microns in size and require highly site specific
 sample preparation.
 FIB specimen preparation has been successfully applied to a range of these
 structures to reveal the nature of the interfaces for both mechanically
 exfoliated 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "haigh2012crosssectional,georgiou2013vertical,wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic,cheng2014fewlayer,liu2015towardbarrier,cao2015quality,withers2015lightemitting,withers2015wse2lightemitting,radha2016molecular2,cui2015multiterminal"

\end_inset

 and liquid exfoliated flakes
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "withers2014heterostructures"

\end_inset

.
 It should be noted that 
\emph on
in situ
\emph default
 manipulation of 2D materials can give similar information to FIB prepared
 cross sectional imaging with the boon of mechanical deformation data being
 recorded in tandem with TEM BF imaging, though no heterostructures have
 yet been investigated with this technique
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "nikiforov2012nanoscale,tang2014nanomechanical,oviedo2015insitu"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The focussed ion beam
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:The-FIB rev"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unlikely history of the FIB seems unconventional when compared to complement
ary techniques such as the TEM and scanning electron microscopy (SEM).
 The liquid-metal ion source span out of electrostatic rocket engine development
 in the early 1960s and are still used in spacecraft micropropulsion systems
 (e.g.
 the LISA Pathfinder
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lisalaser"

\end_inset

) due to their fast response time and fine current control
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wright2015electric"

\end_inset

.
 Further development by various academic institutions lead to the first
 scanning ion microscopes using 25 keV gallium ions to achieve resolutions
 of 0.2 μm
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "orloff1993highresolution"

\end_inset

.
 By the end of the 1980s FIBs were being used routinely in the semiconducting
 industry for failure analysis, circuit modification and lithographic mask
 repair.
 They were relatively simple to construct as a single column, with the high
 quality Wien filtered (i.e.
 energy filtered) beam adaptable to a large range of working distances.
 As such they could be bolted to existing vacuum chambers and readily adapted
 to provide complementary microscopy and surface analysis
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bassim2014recentadvances"

\end_inset

.
 A popular combination was with SEM and became known as a dual-beam instrument,
 which are now produced as commercial units for micromanipulation, cross
 sectional characterisation, serial sectioning characterisation and TEM
 sample preparation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "giannuzzi2005introduction,hernandez-saz2012amethodology,lechner2012improved,schaffer2012samplepreparation,hemamouche2014fibpatterning,burnett2015largevolume"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the FIB, Ga ions are accelerated from a the tip of a liquid metal ion
 source and focussed using electrostatic lenses to form an ion beam.
 This is rastered across the specimen surface in much the same way as SEM,
 however the destructive nature of the ions bombarding the surface causes
 secondary electrons and secondary ions (as well as neutral species) to
 be ejected from the surface.
 These can be used to form SEM-like images, which adds another line of sight
 inside the chamber at an angle complementary to the SEM column.
 The primary function of the FIB is, however, milling and polishing through
 surface sputtering.
 A range of ion energies and currents are used over the course of a FIB
 procedure to control the damage rate and mitigate against milling artefacts.
 When an energetic ion enters the specimen it will collide with a number
 of primary knock-on atoms (PKAs), which will then go on to have secondary
 collisions with many other specimen atoms in what is known as a collision
 cascade
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "was2007fundamentals"

\end_inset

.
 This takes the form of a column of displaced atoms through the specimen
 about the axis of the incident ion
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "opfermann2000formation"

\end_inset

.
 As soon as the primary and secondary atoms have undergone all collisions,
 the column of amorphous material shrinks radially as the crystal structure
 at the column/specimen interface seeds crystal growth.
 The final radius of the amorphous columns can therefore be increased by
 cooling the specimen, or by increasing the ion flux so that column edges
 overlap and eliminate seeding
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gibbons1972ionimplantation,koike1988effectof"

\end_inset

.
 Material at the surface of the specimen is ejected through a similar collision
 mechanism of knock-on atoms, however the accompanying amorphous damage
 at the ion beam site is always present.
 Surface sputtering also directly competes with redeposition of milled material,
 which can be controlled by specimen geometry, beam angle and ion parameters
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "giannuzzi2005introduction"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ion_range_figure.pdf
	width 13.5cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout

