\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of FIB sample preparation for high resolution (S)TEM is to mill
 out a 30-60 nanometre-thick window of pristine specimen from a specific
 site.
 The preparation procedure therefore aims to minimise the thickness of the
 amorphous damage layer, which will inevitably cover the surface of any
 lamella we produce with an ion beam, whilst simultaneously thinning the
 specimen to electron-transparency.
 One final constraint is equipment time: FIB access is very expensive and
 even a fast TEM lamella preparation of an ideal material can take 3 hours.
 Therefore, over the course of a procedure, high energies and currents are
 used to roughly cut out the lamella and transfer it to a TEM grid, and
 then the parameters are gradually stepped down until low energies and currents
 are polishing the lamella surface to minimise the amorphous layer (procedure
 used in this work is detailed in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:FIB-preparation-of"

\end_inset

).
 We can estimate the thickness of the amorphous layer using the popular
 stopping range of ions in matter (SRIM) simulation package by calculating
 the theoretical range of primary and secondary ions in any material
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ziegler2010srim"

\end_inset

.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SRIM-Calc"

\end_inset

 shows an example SRIM calculation plot of various ion ranges for 30 keV
 gallium ions in graphite, along with the longitudinal range for different
 ion energies in this system.
 The range roughly decays by an order of magnitude on going from 30 keV
 to 1 keV, which is the selection of accelerating energies available in
 a typical commercial dual-beam.
 We can use these values to estimate the final thickness of the lamella
 for each step of the sample preparation procudure and modify it accordingly
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "schaffer2012samplepreparation"

\end_inset

, as shown in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:FIB_lamella_planning"

\end_inset

.
 It should be noted that SRIM will underestimate ion ranges as it approximates
 all materials to be homogenous with a constant density, and cannot model
 channelling effects which result in an extended ion range down crystallographic
 axes
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kempshall2001ionchanneling"

\end_inset

.
 To account for this the range is typically multiplied by a correction factor
 of 3-5
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2005surface"

\end_inset

.
 For particularly sensitive materials which may be affected by the electrostatic
 field generated by the ion beam (e.g.
 atomically thin TMDCs, see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:FIB-induced-limits"

\end_inset

), a more conservative factor of 10 should be used.
 It should also be noted that the larger this correction factor the longer
 the FIB procedure will take, and the greater the potential for milling
 artefacts to form.
\end_layout

