\begin_layout Subsubsection
Cross sectional (S)TEM imaging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
High resolution STEM images presented in this thesis were captured using
 a probe side aberration-corrected FEI Titan G2 80-200 kV with an X-FEG
 electron source operated at 200kV in the School of Materials, University
 of Manchester.
 HAADF and BF STEM imaging was performed using a probe convergence angle
 of 21 mrad, a HAADF inner angle of 62 mrad and a probe current in the range
 of 75-90 pA.
 EDXS analysis was performed in the Titan using a Super-X four silicon drift
 EDXS detector system with a total collection solid angle of 0.7 srad.
 EELS was carried out using a Gatan Imaging Filter (GIF) Quantum ER system
 with a 5 mm entrance aperture, a collection angle of 62 mrad and an energy
 dispersion of 1 eV.
 The multilayer structures were oriented along an <hkl0> crystallographic
 direction by taking advantage of the Kukuchi bands of the Si substrate.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "haigh2012crosssectional,withers2015lightemitting,cao2015quality"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Image processing and analysis methods
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Image-processing-methods"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The image processing methods used in chapter 4 are here explained in detail.
 Image processing and analysis was also used in chapters 5 and 6, however
 the procedures there are a lot simpler and detailed in the supplementary
 information section of chapter 5 and in the introduction to chapter 6.
 The paper presented in chapter 4 
\emph on
Twinning in van der Waals Crystals of 2D Materials
\emph default
 used quantitative analysis of HAADF STEM images showing extended twin boundarie
s in graphite, hBN and MoSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 We will go through the image processing procedure for a single image of
 a graphite twin boundary, followed by a demonstration of the kind of analysis
 possible with lattice fringe contrast images.
 For the publication, multiple images for each material were processed and
 analysed in this way.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The aim of the image processing described here is to remove image noise
 such that continuous sections of basal plane can be successful segmented
 (recognised as separate lines by the software algorithm) and all useful
 information is retained.
 Gatan’s Digital Micrograph software was used to apply bandpass (1.5 – 7
 nm
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

) and radial filters to each image’s FFT to remove high frequency noise
 and information not associated with the curved basal planes (see Figure
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:DM im processing"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

