\begin_layout Standard
The slip translation length of each pair of basal planes can be found by
 measuring the difference in length for one plane relative to its neighbour
 in our HAADF STEM images.
 This is achieved by plotting construction lines perpendicular to the basal
 planes either side of the twin boundary, measuring the length of the basal
 plane between the two lines and then calculating the difference in length
 between the two basal planes (see the main text in chapter 4 for results).
 To perform this analysis required the development of further careful image
 processing approaches; which are summarised in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Medial-axis-skeletonization"

\end_inset

.
 To begin, masks were applied to the two regular lattices, well away from
 the central twin boundary and perpendicular to the basal planes in order
 to constrain the region of interest (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Medial-axis-skeletonization"

\end_inset

C).
 Medial axis skeletonization was used to isolate the centre of the basal
 plane: the width of each basal plane was eroded until only a single chain
 of pixels comprising the centre of the plane remained (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Medial-axis-skeletonization"

\end_inset

D,E,F).
 Gaussian filtering (10 pixel width) can help this process by smoothing
 the edges of a basal plane to eliminate branching.
 The number of pixels in a skeleton were counted to find the length of the
 basal plane in pixels.
 The images were then independently calibrated using the bulk lattice basal
 plane spacing from areas of the specimen with normal stacking and free
 from defects.
 This calibration was used to convert the number of pixels in the skeleton
 to the length of the basal plane in nanometres.
 By taking the difference in length of neighbouring skeletonized basal planes,
 the slip translation length can be determined for the twin boundary (see
 chapter 4).
\end_layout

