\begin_layout Standard
The final advantage of segmenting basal planes is that it allows their relative
 separations to be determined, pixel by pixel, and thus the localised interlayer
 strain across the boundary to be measured.
 After filtering, masking, segmentation and medial axis skeletonization,
 the basal plane pixel skeletons were used to generate an interlayer strain
 map.
 For each pixel in a skeleton, a script calculated the nearest twenty pixels
 in a neighbouring skeleton.
 This was repeated for the other neighbouring skeleton line in order to
 get a list of forty neighbour pixels for all planes except those with only
 one neighbour (the top and bottom planes in the image, Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Strain_map"

\end_inset

C).
 A value corresponding to the mean distance to these nearest-neighbour pixels
 was assigned to the primary pixel.
 This process was repeated for all basal planes until every pixel had a
 value corresponding to the average distance to a neighbouring basal plane.
 This distance value 
\emph on
d
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\emph on
pixel
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\emph default
can be simply converted to a percentage interlayer strain by normalising
 it to the bulk interlayer spacing 
\emph on
d
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\emph on
bulk
\end_layout

\end_inset


\emph default
 for the material:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\%\:interlayer\:strain=\frac{d_{pixel}}{d_{bulk}}\times100\%
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
