\begin_layout Standard
It is well known that the FIB beam cannot make a perfect, fully crystalline
 lamella due to the delocalised damaging nature of the ion beam
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bassim2014recentadvances"

\end_inset

.
 The thickness of the amorphous layer can be minimised by using low energy
 ion polishing (2 kV or lower) at progressively lower currents (see section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:FIB-preparation-of"

\end_inset

), however it cannot be eliminated entirely by current methods
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "burnett2015largevolume"

\end_inset

.
 The smearing artefact is in fact due to heterogenous element distribution
 in the amorphous layer of these lamellae which results from their differing
 interaction with the focussed ion beam, as we will now discuss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can simulate the FIB sputtering mechanism 
\emph on
in situ
\emph default
 with electrons in the STEM
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "garcia2014analysis,lin2014flexible"

\end_inset

.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-damage"

\end_inset

B shows a HAADF STEM image of monolayer WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in cross section.
 As the electrons pass through the lamella they damage the WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Electron-beam-induced"

\end_inset

).
 The evolution of this damage can be monitored by acquiring consecutive
 frames of HAADF images (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-damage"

\end_inset

A) and EDXS SI maps of an area and comparing the concentration of different
 elements to the first frame of undamaged area.
 The Cliff-Lorimer equation tells us that the intensity ratio of two x-ray
 peaks is proportional to the relative concentrations of the corresponding
 elements
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cliff1975thequantitative"

\end_inset

.
 By subtracting the background and measuring the peak intensity of an x-ray
 peak (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-damage"

\end_inset

C) over a series of acquisitions, we can determine the changing concentration
 of an element over time relative to the first frame of the undamaged specimen.
 The relative concentrations determined over an increasing fluence for the
 Se Kα and W Lα peaks are plotted in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-damage"

\end_inset

D.
 Examining the Se Kα plot, we see that the relative concentrations decay
 with increased fluence.
 This is the trend anticipated in a knock-on regime, where a collision from
 an incident electron ejects an atom from the material.
 A least squares fit to this data allows us to determine the sputtering
 cross section for this event to be 6.4 barns.
 The W Lα plot does not show behaviour characteristic of knock-on damage;
 the concentration of tungsten actually increases before decaying gradually.
 This can be explained by considering the environment of the imaged area.
 The WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 layer is a thin ribbon of semi-conducting material encased in insulating
 hBN on both sides.
 As the electron beam penetrates the lamella, secondary electrons generated
 from ionised atoms course through the semiconductor, damaging the crystal
 structure outwith the raster of the STEM.
 The effects of this can be seen in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-damage"

\end_inset

B, wherein the WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 lattice has been severely damaged outside of the scan area.
 This in turn leads to free tungsten species able to migrate across or through
 the lamella into the area with highest charge density: the STEM scan area.
 If the beam is allowed to raster longer across this area we see coarsening
 of the tungsten to form crystalline structures within the damaged region
 (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-damage"

\end_inset

B).
 Eventually the extent of the damage becomes limited and the amount of free
 tungsten able to migrate into the area dries up, leading to a gradual decrease
 in tungsten concetration.
\end_layout

