\begin_layout Standard
The step function is of the form
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(x)=\frac{A[1+erf(\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma})]}{2}\label{eq:Step fit func}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\emph on
A
\emph default
 is the amplitude, 
\emph on
μ
\emph default
 is the step centre and
\emph on
 σ 
\emph default
the characteristic width of the step.
 The latter variable is perhaps the most important.
 If we consider the HAADF image in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Characterising-the-smearing"

\end_inset

 as just a snapshot of the FIB polishing process, where the sharpness of
 the step imaged corresponds to the distribution of tungsten across the
 interface: as we polish more and more, the thickness of the disordered
 layers decreases and our interface will sharpen as the spontaneously amorphous
 distribution of tungsten atoms becomes less diffuse with every step of
 our FIB polishing procedure.
 i.e.
 as our FIB polishing becomes finer, the interface becomes more defined.
 In this respect Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step fit func"

\end_inset

 is equivalent to Fick's second law of diffusion, and 
\emph on
σ 
\emph default
characterises the sharpness of the interface which will tend to 1 as the
